-- A dependency's type error does not become the compiler's fault in
-- the module that compares the poisoned value. `A.f` is `<error>` in `A`'s
-- interface; `T` compares it with `==`. A wanted on an `err` receiver is
-- `poisoned`, and the evidence-count assert must not read "no error in
-- THIS module" as "no poison reached it" — that panics the Debug binary
-- ("1 instruction(s) whose evidence tree does not add up") and gives two
-- INTERNAL ERRORs at `T.beni:5:9` in ReleaseFast. Also `A.f < 1`,
-- `[ A.f ] == [ 1 ]`, `List.sort [ A.f ]`, `A.f.eq 1`, and a NAMING ERROR
-- in `A` instead of a type error. Expected: exactly `A`'s one error, and
-- nothing in `T`.
pub f =
    1 + "x"
